And:
>  There is no proposition such that only one
> utterance maps to it. To prove that, just find the sentence uttered,
> and utter it again.

Many sentences will change meaning when uttered for a second time.
I would even say that every sentence will have a different meaning
each time that it is uttered, but probably you kick that difference
out to pragmatics.

> I don't see how different utterances of {da de zehe gerku} correspond
> to different propositions. I am talking about propositions derivable
> by grammatical rule, not by ad hoc inference.

Yes, I think that is where I misunderstood. I meant that either each
utterance maps to a (sometimes only very slightly) different proposition,
or propositions are not all that there is to language. I suppose you
meant the second one.

